IDXDUPES is a simple program that is used to scan your PCBoard .IDX files for
duplicate files based on filenames which may appear to have the same "root"
name with changes made either to the filename extension or to the end of the
filename as in revision-level naming.

Here is an example of the output IDXDUPES may produce:

   AWARD100.ZIP   H:\DOORUPLD\         note that the root is the same and
   AWARD105.ZIP   H:\DOORUPLD\         only the version number is different

   NEWS320 .ZIP   H:\DOOR14\           here the version number is followed by
   NEWS320A.ZIP   H:\UTIL145\          a letter but it is still found

   NR201CVT.ZIP   H:\UTIL14\           these all appear related and IDXDUPES
   NR205   .ZIP   H:\UTIL145\          groups them all together, NR205 was the
   NR206   .ZIP   H:\UTIL145\          one that needed to be removed

   RM-150A .ZIP   H:\DOOR145\          as it turns out these are NOT duplicates
   RM-150B .ZIP   H:\DOOR145\          they are 4 "parts" of the same posting,
   RM-150C .ZIP   H:\DOOR145\          however, they were still reported by
   RM-150D .ZIP   H:\DOOR145\          IDXDUPES for sysop review

   FILE10.ZIP     H:\UTIL14\           Here the ZIP and ARJ extensions are
   FILE10.ARJ     H:\UTIL14\           shown to have no effect on IDXDUPES'
   FILE10A.ZIP    H:\UTILUPLD\         ability to locate duplicates

It is very possible for files to have similar filenames yet have no relation
one to another.  It is also possible, as shown above, for filenames to have
extensions which indicate that a group of files "belong together" rather than
that one is an updated version of the other.  Therefore, it is recommended that
you not ASSUME that all files found are duplicates.  This program is supplied
merely to reduce the mass of filenames down to a more manageable list of
"possible duplicates" that a sysop could then consider removing.

It is recommended that you use PCBFiler to review the description of each file
found and possibly even view the contents to verify that one file duplicates
or updates another before removing one or the other.

One very obvious limitation to this program is that the .IDX files that PCBoard
uses are limited to 65,535 files each.  While PCBoard is capable of making use
of *several* IDX files at once, this program is not - it will work with only a
single .IDX file at a time.

Furthermore, PCBoard generally requires that upload files be excluded from the
.IDX file.  In order to catch the most number of duplicates it is recommended
that you create a special .IDX file that includes not just your download paths
but your upload paths as well then let IDXDUPES search this all encompassing
.IDX file instead of the standard one that PCBoard uses.

Usage is as follows:

    MAKEIDX ALLFILES.PTH ALLFILES.IDX       <-  create a new .IDX file
    IDXDUPES ALLFILES.IDX > DUPES.LST       <-  create dupes.lst

The program sends its output to standard out and DOS redirection can be used,
as shown above, to capture the output to a file.

This program is provided free of charge as is.
